26,2,,status,Shizue,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
27,2,,status,May,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
28,2,,status,Midori,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
29,2,,status,Benjamin,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
30,2,1,talk,May,,"It seems some of our past efforts were more successful than others. One pilot actually met its cost-recovery target, though the accessibility results were a bit mixed regarding lane priority.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
31,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,My findings are concerning; the late-turn pool is thinning and we are relying heavily on overtime for both final departures and overnight works. We are understaffed in key crew areas.,"{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
32,2,1,talk,Midori,,"Interestingly, the mid-city runs are slower than they used to be, but the crowds are clearing at the same rate. It seems the delays aren't where we typically assume they are.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
33,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"I've discovered that bidders are pricing in heavy contingencies for work in pre-war shells due to unrecorded voids, and there's a lack of agreement on passenger data terms between ferry and rail.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
34,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"The financial picture is quite strained; we have a ring-fenced construction balance from a lapsed grant, but our operating subsidy and technology lines have been trimmed again.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
35,2,2,talk,May,,"@Benjamin, if the staffing is that precarious, does that mean any plan requiring more late-night or overnight work is essentially a non-starter right now?","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"Precisely, May. Unless a plan includes a significant recruitment drive or a way to reduce the burden on the late-turn pool, we are simply building on a foundation of exhaustion.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Shizue, does the lack of agreement on data terms mean that integrating different services would be a bureaucratic nightmare, or just a slow process?","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"It feels more like a fundamental disagreement than a slow process, Midori. If the back offices cannot agree on the terms, the technical integration is the least of our worries.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@May, the financial constraints I found suggest we should be very careful about any plan that increases our long-term operating costs, especially since the technology budget has been cut.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,3,talk,May,,"@Jonathan, that makes the integrated fare system look risky if the technology budget is shrinking. @Midori, did your audits show if passengers are actually struggling with the ticketing machines?","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Jonathan"",""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@May, I'm more concerned that any plan involving physical construction or new routes will clash with our current maintenance crew shortages. We can't maintain what we can't staff.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,3,talk,Midori,,"Actually, the audits show that queues at the machines rarely last beyond a minute. The real time loss is happening while passengers are waiting for their connections, not during the payment process.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,3,talk,Shizue,,"@Benjamin, if the maintenance crews are so stretched, I wonder if the risks associated with those pre-war station shells make any structural upgrades far more dangerous than they appear on paper.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree with Shizue. If we are already facing budget trims and staffing shortages, taking on high-risk construction in those old shells could lead to a financial sinkhole we cannot afford.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,4,talk,May,,"It sounds like we're narrowing it down. If the ticketing machines aren't the bottleneck and the staffing is this fragile, we should be very cautious about any plan that adds operational pressure.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,I agree. We need a solution that doesn't rely on a workforce that is already at its breaking point or require overnight works that we cannot realistically staff.,"{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,4,talk,Midori,,"If the delays are actually in the connections rather than the payment process, perhaps we should focus on improvements that streamline the passenger flow without adding too much operational stress.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,4,talk,Shizue,,"I agree with Midori. We must avoid plans that ignore the hidden risks of our infrastructure or the friction between our operators, as those are the things that truly stall our progress.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"Given the budget cuts and the staffing crisis Benjamin highlighted, we must prioritize a plan that is financially sustainable and doesn't overextend our remaining personnel.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,3,,status,Jonathan,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
51,3,,status,Shizue,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
52,3,,status,May,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
53,3,,status,Midori,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
54,3,,status,Benjamin,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
55,3,1,talk,May,,"Now that we see the candidates, the Integrated Fare Platform seems unnecessary since ticketing isn't the bottleneck, and the Night Service Extension is impossible given Benjamin's staffing findings.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,I agree with May. The Night Service Extension is a fantasy when the late-turn pool is already exhausted. That leaves us with the Bus Corridor or the Station Access Upgrades.,"{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,3,1,talk,Midori,,"The Station Access Upgrades might help with the connection delays I found, but @Shizue, wouldn't those upgrades likely involve the risky pre-war shells you mentioned?","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"Exactly, Midori. Those upgrades would almost certainly involve the pre-war shells, and with the unrecorded voids I discovered, the risk of delays and cost overruns is far too high.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,That leaves the Crosstown Bus Corridor. It avoids the structural risks of the old stations and doesn't seem to place the same impossible burden on our late-night staffing as the extension would.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,2,talk,May,,"I'm inclined to agree with Jonathan. The Bus Corridor seems the most pragmatic choice, especially since we know from the accessibility records that lane-priority can be tricky, but it's still safer than the alternatives.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,I'm on board with the Bus Corridor. It's the only option that doesn't actively ignore the staffing crisis or gamble with the structural integrity of the network.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,2,talk,Midori,,"I agree. While the Bus Corridor has its challenges, it addresses the mid-city delays I noticed without the extreme risks associated with the other candidates.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"I agree as well. It avoids the bureaucratic friction of the fare platform and the physical dangers of the station shells, making it the most stable path forward.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"It seems we have reached a consensus. The Crosstown Bus Corridor is the only plan that aligns with our financial constraints, staffing realities, and risk tolerance.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,3,talk,May,,I'll cast my vote for the Crosstown Bus Corridor. It's the most sensible option given everything we've discussed.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,My vote is also for the Crosstown Bus Corridor. It is the only logical choice that respects our current operational limits.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,3,talk,Midori,,I vote for the Crosstown Bus Corridor as well. It's the most balanced approach to improving the network.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,3,talk,Shizue,,I also vote for the Crosstown Bus Corridor. It is the safest and most considerate choice for our future.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,I vote for the Crosstown Bus Corridor. It is the most fiscally responsible and sustainable option available to us.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
